# Line endings, decided once so no tool can decide differently.
#
# Before this file the repository was inconsistent: 16 files pure LF, 7 pure
# CRLF and 12 mixed, with no rule anywhere. Any tool that rewrote a file
# normalised it to the platform default and put dozens of whitespace-only lines
# in the diff, burying the real change.
#
# `text=auto` stores every text file with LF and lets Git detect binaries;
# `eol=lf` also writes LF into the working tree, so the choice survives a
# checkout on Windows instead of being undone by it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF wherever they are checked out. The kernel reads the
# shebang literally, so a CRLF makes it `/bin/sh\r` and the script dies with
# "bad interpreter". These are the Git hooks — the secret scan and the unit-test
# gate — so the failure would be silent exactly where it matters most.
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/pre-commit text eol=lf
.githooks/commit-msg text eol=lf

# Binary, never touched. Git's own detection already gets this right; the rule
# is explicit because a mistake here corrupts the image in a way that is not
# obvious until someone opens it.
*.png binary
